WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.297 --> 00:00:03.649
(crowd cheering)

00:00:03.649 --> 00:00:04.814
- Have y'all ever played together?

00:00:04.814 --> 00:00:05.880
Have you ever played with Sheila?

00:00:05.880 --> 00:00:06.930
- I didn't realize that's who I was

00:00:06.930 --> 00:00:08.070
in the makeup room with.

00:00:08.070 --> 00:00:08.903
- Oh my gosh.

00:00:08.903 --> 00:00:11.340
I should've been like, you
wanna come out and play with us?

00:00:11.340 --> 00:00:12.660
I didn't even think of
that, but I was like,

00:00:12.660 --> 00:00:13.890
man, y'all would sound incredible.

00:00:13.890 --> 00:00:16.291
You're both such amazing
musicians and artists. Yeah.

00:00:16.291 --> 00:00:17.520
- Would've just held her
sticks till she needed 'em.

00:00:17.520 --> 00:00:19.440
- Oh my God. She is incredible.

00:00:19.440 --> 00:00:22.087
But I have to ask you, first
of all, this is amazing.

00:00:22.087 --> 00:00:22.920
- Thank you.

00:00:22.920 --> 00:00:24.338
- I'm gonna steal it.

00:00:24.338 --> 00:00:27.060
But did you just go to
Japan with your son?

00:00:27.060 --> 00:00:27.893
- I did.

00:00:27.893 --> 00:00:28.726
- I love Japan.

00:00:28.726 --> 00:00:29.920
- I love Japan.

00:00:29.920 --> 00:00:30.753
- [Kelly] Did you have such a good time?

00:00:30.753 --> 00:00:32.370
- You know, I've been there
before, probably like you,

00:00:32.370 --> 00:00:34.530
but I've never gotten
to see or look around.

00:00:34.530 --> 00:00:35.550
'cause we sing

00:00:35.550 --> 00:00:36.549
- We're working.

00:00:36.549 --> 00:00:37.382
- We do interviews all day.

00:00:37.382 --> 00:00:39.745
So I went for pure vacation.
We had the best time.

00:00:39.745 --> 00:00:42.708
- Yeah. First of all, the
strawberry blonde is so your vibe.

00:00:42.708 --> 00:00:44.078
- Thank you. Thank you.

00:00:44.078 --> 00:00:45.210
- Yeah. I have to compliment it.

00:00:45.210 --> 00:00:49.170
So your son, my kids, they
asked, they're 7 and 9,

00:00:49.170 --> 00:00:50.610
but they asked to come on stage with me.

00:00:50.610 --> 00:00:52.590
Did your son, 'cause I
know that he plays with you

00:00:52.590 --> 00:00:53.970
sometimes on stage, right? He's 12, right?

00:00:53.970 --> 00:00:55.680
- Yeah. He plays some drums

00:00:55.680 --> 00:00:58.440
and he asked to come out on tour with me

00:00:58.440 --> 00:00:59.910
and we had practiced really good.

00:00:59.910 --> 00:01:02.160
And then he went to his
dad's for two weeks in Texas

00:01:02.160 --> 00:01:04.800
and he got out and we
show up to soundcheck

00:01:04.800 --> 00:01:06.810
and y'all, he wasn't good.

00:01:06.810 --> 00:01:08.100
You were just here. Oh look!

00:01:08.100 --> 00:01:10.536
- You're like, oh bud, did
you take some time off?

00:01:10.536 --> 00:01:11.700
- Well, it was my fault

00:01:11.700 --> 00:01:14.610
'cause I didn't rehearse
with him prior to soundcheck.

00:01:14.610 --> 00:01:16.380
And so it was one of those
weird moments as a mom

00:01:16.380 --> 00:01:19.607
where you're like, ooh, how do
I break the news? This isn't.

00:01:19.607 --> 00:01:20.513
- Yeah. But be honest.

00:01:21.524 --> 00:01:22.470
'cause you know, it's like you'd rather,

00:01:22.470 --> 00:01:23.550
I mean, my mother wasn't mean.

00:01:23.550 --> 00:01:25.230
It's like, you gotta be
honest with your kids.

00:01:25.230 --> 00:01:26.670
That way they don't go
out there and do something

00:01:26.670 --> 00:01:28.380
and they're like, mom,
why didn't you tell me?

00:01:28.380 --> 00:01:29.213
- Exactly.

00:01:29.213 --> 00:01:30.660
- Yeah. He had a moment though. Right?

00:01:30.660 --> 00:01:32.280
So it didn't work out.

00:01:32.280 --> 00:01:33.660
But then he kind of went to the bathroom

00:01:33.660 --> 00:01:35.190
and did you overhear
him talking to himself?

00:01:35.190 --> 00:01:36.741
Is that what you?

00:01:36.741 --> 00:01:37.574
- Yeah, so I told him, I was
like, you know, first of all,

00:01:37.574 --> 00:01:39.180
I should have practiced
with you before today

00:01:39.180 --> 00:01:41.760
and you're not quite ready for tonight,

00:01:41.760 --> 00:01:43.590
but we can practice before tonight

00:01:43.590 --> 00:01:44.610
and if you get there we can do it.

00:01:44.610 --> 00:01:46.800
And if not, we have months on tour.

00:01:46.800 --> 00:01:48.390
And he's like, no, I wanna
practice. I wanna try.

00:01:48.390 --> 00:01:50.190
And I was like, all right.
So we start practicing

00:01:50.190 --> 00:01:52.210
and he misses his entrance.

00:01:52.210 --> 00:01:53.190
We do it again, misses his entrance,

00:01:53.190 --> 00:01:54.690
do it again, misses his entrance.

00:01:54.690 --> 00:01:57.690
And he's like, it's cool.
It's cool. I'll be right back.

00:01:57.690 --> 00:01:59.880
And he goes into the bathroom
and he shuts the door

00:01:59.880 --> 00:02:00.727
and I hear him go

00:02:00.727 --> 00:02:05.727
"Here I am, crying in the
bathroom. (crowd laughs)

00:02:07.107 --> 00:02:08.880
And I know I'm not supposed to be crying

00:02:08.880 --> 00:02:11.820
'cause it's okay to make mistakes

00:02:11.820 --> 00:02:14.640
but it doesn't feel very good."

00:02:14.640 --> 00:02:17.287
And then it gets quiet for a
minute. And then he's like,

00:02:17.287 --> 00:02:19.020
"Well," and then he opens the door

00:02:19.020 --> 00:02:19.920
and I run back to the bed.

00:02:19.920 --> 00:02:22.020
I'm like, cool, cool. Everything
cool? What's going on?

00:02:22.020 --> 00:02:23.490
And he's like, let's do it again.

00:02:23.490 --> 00:02:25.110
And we kept practicing.

00:02:25.110 --> 00:02:26.280
He practiced for a long time

00:02:26.280 --> 00:02:27.960
and he got it to where
I was like, if you want,

00:02:27.960 --> 00:02:30.060
you can try it tonight.

00:02:30.060 --> 00:02:32.130
So he walked out there
and he just crushed it.

00:02:32.130 --> 00:02:33.708
It was pretty cool.

00:02:33.708 --> 00:02:35.430
- I love that. (crowd applauding)

00:02:35.430 --> 00:02:38.430
Did you teach him that
kind of emotional maturity?

00:02:38.430 --> 00:02:41.280
Because that's pretty mature
for a 12 year old kid.

00:02:41.280 --> 00:02:44.040
Just to walk in there and
walk yourself through it.

00:02:44.040 --> 00:02:45.990
- The best thing I think for me as a mom

00:02:45.990 --> 00:02:48.930
has just been learning to
heal and heal my own self.

00:02:48.930 --> 00:02:51.420
And it's made me a much
more available mom.

00:02:51.420 --> 00:02:52.260
- [Kelly] Yeah. Amen.

00:02:52.260 --> 00:02:55.740
- And I know so much more
now than I was taught.

00:02:55.740 --> 00:02:58.020
And so yeah, teaching him emotional tools,

00:02:58.020 --> 00:02:59.970
learning how to label the right feeling.

00:02:59.970 --> 00:03:01.740
Because a lot of us,
I don't know if y'all,

00:03:01.740 --> 00:03:03.780
I grew up with emotional dyslexia.

00:03:03.780 --> 00:03:05.970
I was like, not treated very nice.

00:03:05.970 --> 00:03:07.200
And it was like, I love you.

00:03:07.200 --> 00:03:09.240
It's the wrong word for those things.

00:03:09.240 --> 00:03:11.661
You know, so you kind
of match miss feelings.

00:03:11.661 --> 00:03:12.840
- Because you're like,
what does love mean?

00:03:12.840 --> 00:03:14.940
- I'm gonna go get in some
bad relationships for a while.

00:03:14.940 --> 00:03:15.960
Figure it out.

00:03:15.960 --> 00:03:17.460
- I don't know what you speak of.

00:03:17.460 --> 00:03:20.070
Okay. So, you toured with your parents,

00:03:20.070 --> 00:03:22.200
which I didn't know that.
Both were musicians.

00:03:22.200 --> 00:03:23.880
So you toured at a very young age, right?

00:03:23.880 --> 00:03:25.440
- Yeah, I started touring with them.

00:03:25.440 --> 00:03:28.800
I started singing with them
at 5 in hotels for tourists.

00:03:28.800 --> 00:03:31.290
And then we would tour
Alaska Native villages,

00:03:31.290 --> 00:03:33.960
Inuit villages, all through
like the Arctic Circle.

00:03:33.960 --> 00:03:34.800
- That's cool.

00:03:34.800 --> 00:03:37.320
- Yeah. So we get dropped
off on a plane on skis

00:03:37.320 --> 00:03:39.150
and then a dog sled would pick us up.

00:03:39.150 --> 00:03:41.190
That was our transpo. We,

00:03:41.190 --> 00:03:42.724
- This is not normal.

00:03:42.724 --> 00:03:45.750
- No. We would stay in a
cabin with like a host family.

00:03:45.750 --> 00:03:48.240
And I remember being given
a barbecued moose leg,

00:03:48.240 --> 00:03:51.753
it was like this long. And I was like,

00:03:53.779 --> 00:03:55.710
- You're like, how do you approach it?

00:03:55.710 --> 00:03:57.900
How do you think you go in on that?

00:03:57.900 --> 00:03:58.733
- Full caveman with it.

00:03:58.733 --> 00:04:01.470
- A little bigger than a
drumstick. That's amazing.

00:04:01.470 --> 00:04:03.030
So You're Not Alone Challenge,

00:04:03.030 --> 00:04:04.740
let's talk about, we
talked about this before.

00:04:04.740 --> 00:04:06.060
But it came back around this year.

00:04:06.060 --> 00:04:08.580
So it's again happening. What
do you wanna tell people?

00:04:08.580 --> 00:04:10.740
What if they haven't heard
of Not Alone Challenge?

00:04:10.740 --> 00:04:12.547
- Last year was our first time doing the

00:04:12.547 --> 00:04:13.497
#notalonechallenge.

00:04:14.340 --> 00:04:15.810
You guys helped me debut.

00:04:15.810 --> 00:04:16.720
- [Kelly] On the show.

00:04:16.720 --> 00:04:17.553
- And thanks to you guys, we became

00:04:17.553 --> 00:04:20.100
the biggest social media
campaign for mental health.

00:04:20.100 --> 00:04:20.933
- I don't think it was us,

00:04:20.933 --> 00:04:22.123
but I think maybe you had
a lot to do with that.

00:04:22.123 --> 00:04:24.600
- I think you had a huge
bit of to do with that.

00:04:24.600 --> 00:04:27.630
We reached one and a half
billion eyeballs last year.

00:04:27.630 --> 00:04:28.846
- What?

00:04:28.846 --> 00:04:30.540
- And it really shows
I think the importance

00:04:30.540 --> 00:04:33.690
and the need of social anxiety.

00:04:33.690 --> 00:04:35.490
Of different mental health issues.

00:04:35.490 --> 00:04:37.650
And it's really important
to raise awareness.

00:04:37.650 --> 00:04:39.510
But what we really wanna
do with this challenge

00:04:39.510 --> 00:04:42.690
is make sure we're putting
tools into people's hands.

00:04:42.690 --> 00:04:45.630
'cause we're about
500,000 therapists short

00:04:45.630 --> 00:04:46.920
currently in America.

00:04:46.920 --> 00:04:49.920
And there's lots of tools
that work like CBT and DBT

00:04:49.920 --> 00:04:52.200
or learning how to meditate
in different tools.

00:04:52.200 --> 00:04:53.310
There's a lot of people that live in

00:04:53.310 --> 00:04:55.050
what I call a mental health desert

00:04:55.050 --> 00:04:57.360
where they just don't
have access or money.

00:04:57.360 --> 00:04:58.590
And that's unacceptable.

00:04:58.590 --> 00:05:01.890
One in four kids right now
are having suicidal ideation.

00:05:01.890 --> 00:05:04.140
That's not a number we
intend to live with.

00:05:04.140 --> 00:05:06.030
We can really do something about that.

00:05:06.030 --> 00:05:07.753
- Absolutely, oh my God.

00:05:07.753 --> 00:05:09.660
- Yeah. So we're doing auction
items if people wanna see,

00:05:09.660 --> 00:05:12.300
I mean, last year Billie Eilish
did an incredible package.

00:05:12.300 --> 00:05:14.631
All sorts of athletes, if they go to

00:05:14.631 --> 00:05:17.070
NotAloneChallenge.org,
they can see what to.

00:05:17.070 --> 00:05:18.600
- I was gonna say, how
else can people help?

00:05:18.600 --> 00:05:19.920
Is that the thing? Bidding on things.

00:05:19.920 --> 00:05:22.470
- Yeah, bidding on things
really helps. That raises money.

00:05:22.470 --> 00:05:25.350
The Foundation Reserves Social
Club is actually gonna match

00:05:25.350 --> 00:05:27.510
donations up to a million dollars.

00:05:27.510 --> 00:05:30.690
So every dollar y'all give
really, really counts.

00:05:30.690 --> 00:05:33.210
And this is again, empowering,
especially at-risk youth

00:05:33.210 --> 00:05:34.770
to have mental health help.

00:05:34.770 --> 00:05:37.610
- Absolutely. Which is our future.

00:05:37.610 --> 00:05:38.907
(crowd cheering)

00:05:38.907 --> 00:05:39.906
This year marks the 25th anniversary

00:05:39.906 --> 00:05:42.810
of her multi-platinum album
Spirit, which is incredible.

00:05:42.810 --> 00:05:44.670
So this was your second album, right?

00:05:44.670 --> 00:05:45.503
- Yeah.

00:05:45.503 --> 00:05:47.100
- Did you have that sophomore
pressure? Did you feel it?

00:05:47.100 --> 00:05:47.933
- Oh my god.

00:05:47.933 --> 00:05:48.930
Everybody put that pressure on you.

00:05:48.930 --> 00:05:51.030
I wouldn't have known
about the sophomore slump.

00:05:51.030 --> 00:05:53.310
And you're like, well,
thanks for telling me. Geez.

00:05:53.310 --> 00:05:55.770
- I know people do tell you, why?

00:05:55.770 --> 00:05:57.900
- I don't know. But it
was one of those things

00:05:57.900 --> 00:05:59.010
I really had to contend with.

00:05:59.010 --> 00:06:01.680
Like my first album, I
wrote it not meaning to,

00:06:01.680 --> 00:06:04.257
I just wrote. And my first hit,

00:06:04.257 --> 00:06:06.390
Who Will Save Your Soul is
the first song I ever wrote.

00:06:06.390 --> 00:06:07.873
I didn't mean to write it.

00:06:07.873 --> 00:06:09.660
And I didn't mean to write
a hit. So it was weird

00:06:09.660 --> 00:06:11.902
to be like, how do I now try to do this?

00:06:11.902 --> 00:06:12.930
- Well, it was also very
different. Like your sound

00:06:12.930 --> 00:06:14.460
is very different and
it was very different

00:06:14.460 --> 00:06:15.570
from anything out.

00:06:15.570 --> 00:06:16.650
So I thought that was really cool.

00:06:16.650 --> 00:06:17.640
That's why you probably didn't think, oh,

00:06:17.640 --> 00:06:19.230
I don't know if the masses are gonna.

00:06:19.230 --> 00:06:20.613
But boy. Did we ever.

00:06:21.810 --> 00:06:23.210
Everybody leaned in on that.

00:06:24.082 --> 00:06:24.915
- That's so nice. I know.

00:06:24.915 --> 00:06:28.170
And that was the thing that
really saved me was I had read,

00:06:28.170 --> 00:06:29.580
I got discovered when I was homeless,

00:06:29.580 --> 00:06:31.470
and I'd read a book about
the music business called

00:06:31.470 --> 00:06:33.120
Everything You Need To Know
About the Music Business.

00:06:33.120 --> 00:06:34.950
I got it at the library.

00:06:34.950 --> 00:06:37.260
And I read about contracts and royalties

00:06:37.260 --> 00:06:38.880
and mechanicals and advances.

00:06:38.880 --> 00:06:40.470
And so I turned down my advance.

00:06:40.470 --> 00:06:42.570
I turned down a million
dollars as a homeless kid

00:06:42.570 --> 00:06:45.390
and I took the biggest backend
anybody had ever been given

00:06:45.390 --> 00:06:47.460
because I didn't think a
folk record would sell.

00:06:47.460 --> 00:06:49.560
But if it did sell, I would get rewarded.

00:06:49.560 --> 00:06:52.140
- You are the smartest person alive.

00:06:52.140 --> 00:06:54.870
- And I had a clause in it for
every million albums I sold,

00:06:54.870 --> 00:06:56.580
I would go up by points.

00:06:56.580 --> 00:06:59.701
I sold 15 million albums
on that first album.

00:06:59.701 --> 00:07:01.101
- Oh my God.
- It's insane.

00:07:02.370 --> 00:07:06.118
- So basically you didn't
have to work again.

00:07:06.118 --> 00:07:07.815
You didn't have to work again.

00:07:07.815 --> 00:07:10.369
- And I never cared about
fame. I never cared about hits,

00:07:10.369 --> 00:07:11.202
you know, to write.

00:07:11.202 --> 00:07:12.035
- But you know what you did,
you believed in yourself,

00:07:12.035 --> 00:07:12.868
you believed in your gift.

00:07:12.868 --> 00:07:14.250
And I think that's more,
I've turned down money too

00:07:14.250 --> 00:07:15.270
when they ask you to do things.

00:07:15.270 --> 00:07:18.030
And it's not, but then it also tells you,

00:07:18.030 --> 00:07:19.470
if I'm turning down this much money,

00:07:19.470 --> 00:07:22.260
does that not tell you something like,

00:07:22.260 --> 00:07:23.093
you know what I'm saying?

00:07:23.093 --> 00:07:24.213
I really don't want to do it.

00:07:25.092 --> 00:07:26.970
Like if it's that much money,
I'm really not into that.

00:07:26.970 --> 00:07:28.800
Like that should tell
you. But they don't care.

00:07:28.800 --> 00:07:30.870
- I just really wanted
the artistic freedom.

00:07:30.870 --> 00:07:31.860
And I wanted to earn it.

00:07:31.860 --> 00:07:35.340
I didn't want to like have
this risk on the front end.

00:07:35.340 --> 00:07:37.110
And so that was the exact attitude.

00:07:37.110 --> 00:07:38.340
I was like, wait a minute, I just,

00:07:38.340 --> 00:07:40.530
if I never spend my money,
if I'm really careful,

00:07:40.530 --> 00:07:42.240
I never have to have a hit.

00:07:42.240 --> 00:07:44.355
I don't have to do this, I get to do this.

00:07:44.355 --> 00:07:45.647
And that's really powerful.

00:07:45.647 --> 00:07:47.040
- And then it still stays
your passion and your love.

00:07:47.040 --> 00:07:47.873
- Yeah.

00:07:47.873 --> 00:07:49.740
And so I wrote another weird song

00:07:49.740 --> 00:07:51.150
where the entire bridge was like,

00:07:51.150 --> 00:07:52.650
in the end, only kindness matters.

00:07:52.650 --> 00:07:54.060
The label's like, that's the whole bridge?

00:07:54.060 --> 00:07:55.440
I'm like, yeah, that's the whole bridge.

00:07:55.440 --> 00:07:56.910
Like, it's only all you
wanna say? I'm like, yep.

00:07:56.910 --> 00:07:59.467
Only kindness matters.
And they're like, okay.

00:07:59.467 --> 00:08:02.160
- It's enough. It's a really great line.

00:08:02.160 --> 00:08:03.300
I was like, it's enough.

00:08:03.300 --> 00:08:04.590
So wait, I have to talk to you about this

00:08:04.590 --> 00:08:08.550
because bonus songs on
the 25th inspired by,

00:08:08.550 --> 00:08:10.860
like this, you only got these songs.

00:08:10.860 --> 00:08:12.480
There are songs that you
had recorded in your life,

00:08:12.480 --> 00:08:14.640
or not recorded, but just played.

00:08:14.640 --> 00:08:16.611
And there were fans that actually recorded

00:08:16.611 --> 00:08:19.272
because you didn't remember them.

00:08:19.272 --> 00:08:20.333
- Yeah.

00:08:20.333 --> 00:08:21.600
- And you actually found a fan
you go to, is his name Allen?

00:08:21.600 --> 00:08:22.433
- Yeah.

00:08:22.433 --> 00:08:24.720
- This is the most incredible
story. Tell everybody.

00:08:24.720 --> 00:08:26.220
- So I've always been prolific,

00:08:26.220 --> 00:08:27.540
but I've never written anything down.

00:08:27.540 --> 00:08:29.370
And so I lose songs all the time.

00:08:29.370 --> 00:08:31.320
And so it was the dawn of the internet.

00:08:31.320 --> 00:08:32.970
And so I'd get on there on some listserv.

00:08:32.970 --> 00:08:35.190
I'd be like, does anybody
have a song called Jessica

00:08:35.190 --> 00:08:38.220
that I sang once at a cafe in Toronto?

00:08:38.220 --> 00:08:39.930
And this guy would always write back

00:08:39.930 --> 00:08:41.250
and go, yeah, I have that bootleg.

00:08:41.250 --> 00:08:43.530
And yes, I have that one.
And yes, I have that one.

00:08:43.530 --> 00:08:46.710
I was like, do you want a
job? So I ended up hiring him.

00:08:46.710 --> 00:08:49.800
He's worked for me since
like '94 or something.

00:08:49.800 --> 00:08:50.800
- That's incredible.

00:08:52.470 --> 00:08:55.560
So Jewel is definitely
a lyricist and a poet.

00:08:55.560 --> 00:08:58.980
And so in honor of the 25th
anniversary of her album Spirit,

00:08:58.980 --> 00:09:01.200
we wanted to go back
through some of her lyrics.

00:09:01.200 --> 00:09:05.010
So here's the first. It's
from Hands. Beautiful.

00:09:05.010 --> 00:09:06.330
Which is on Spirit.

00:09:06.330 --> 00:09:08.310
My hands are small, I know

00:09:08.310 --> 00:09:11.280
But they're not yours, they are my own,

00:09:11.280 --> 00:09:12.960
But they're not yours, they are my own,

00:09:12.960 --> 00:09:15.570
and I am never broken. It's so simple.

00:09:15.570 --> 00:09:17.310
Like even your bridge, you
said about the kindness,

00:09:17.310 --> 00:09:20.415
it's so simple, but that's powerful.

00:09:20.415 --> 00:09:23.460
What made you like write
that and stick with that?

00:09:23.460 --> 00:09:24.870
- You know, I wrote a lot of those lyrics

00:09:24.870 --> 00:09:27.570
when I was homeless and
I was shoplifting a lot

00:09:27.570 --> 00:09:30.020
and I was trying to teach
myself not to shoplift.

00:09:30.930 --> 00:09:33.840
And people weren't very nice
to me at that time in my life.

00:09:33.840 --> 00:09:35.460
- Maybe because you're shoplifting or?

00:09:35.460 --> 00:09:37.411
- Yeah. I mean it wasn't a great,

00:09:37.411 --> 00:09:39.870
it wasn't a great life plan for sure.

00:09:39.870 --> 00:09:42.360
But literally that lyric
was like, these are mine.

00:09:42.360 --> 00:09:43.710
Nobody's making me steal.

00:09:43.710 --> 00:09:45.750
I'm choosing to do this
even though I'm homeless,

00:09:45.750 --> 00:09:47.340
even though I have no money,
even though I have no food,

00:09:47.340 --> 00:09:49.980
this is still my choice
and I can do better.

00:09:49.980 --> 00:09:50.910
And I'm not broken,

00:09:50.910 --> 00:09:52.770
but it really came from
this feeling of ownership.

00:09:52.770 --> 00:09:54.300
I thought it was, yeah, exactly.

00:09:54.300 --> 00:09:56.820
- Yeah. I love that. I
didn't mean to make the joke,

00:09:56.820 --> 00:09:58.140
but I had to go there.

00:09:58.140 --> 00:09:59.430
Okay, so wait, one more.

00:09:59.430 --> 00:10:03.151
This is from Intuition and this
is off Jewel's fifth album.

00:10:03.151 --> 00:10:06.630
So follow your heart, your intuition,

00:10:06.630 --> 00:10:08.790
it will lead you in the right direction.

00:10:08.790 --> 00:10:11.910
Let go of your mind. Your
intuition is easy to find.

00:10:11.910 --> 00:10:13.500
Just follow your heart, baby.

00:10:13.500 --> 00:10:15.450
- I feel like the choruses
are never the poetic part,

00:10:15.450 --> 00:10:17.580
actually now that I'm looking at them.

00:10:17.580 --> 00:10:20.820
For me, that song was, I
wanted to write a pop album.

00:10:20.820 --> 00:10:23.670
And it's very, very
hard to write a pop hit

00:10:23.670 --> 00:10:25.050
without talking about love.

00:10:25.050 --> 00:10:27.120
And I counted how many
times it's been done

00:10:27.120 --> 00:10:28.290
and it's very, very few.

00:10:28.290 --> 00:10:30.744
And so I challenged myself
to write a pop song,

00:10:30.744 --> 00:10:31.775
- Unless you're hating on it.

00:10:31.775 --> 00:10:34.950
- Yeah. I guess that's, yeah. Same thing.

00:10:34.950 --> 00:10:37.350
- Because that's my catalog. Yeah.

00:10:37.350 --> 00:10:39.240
- Good catalog, it's been working.

00:10:39.240 --> 00:10:41.276
- So you got a theme.

00:10:41.276 --> 00:10:42.570
- Yeah. So for me it was
like, how do I write a song

00:10:42.570 --> 00:10:43.590
that's not about love?

00:10:43.590 --> 00:10:45.599
That's about like
following your instincts.

00:10:45.599 --> 00:10:47.400
So that was why I wrote it.

00:10:47.400 --> 00:10:50.430
- I love that. So the
25th anniversary edition

00:10:50.430 --> 00:10:53.430
of Jewel's multi-platinum
sophomore album Spirit

00:10:53.430 --> 00:10:54.990
is out November 17th.

00:10:54.990 --> 00:10:57.570
And go online to learn more
about her second annual

00:10:57.570 --> 00:11:00.150
Not Alone Challenge and
how you can participate

00:11:00.150 --> 00:11:01.770
and also how you can
just get some information

00:11:01.770 --> 00:11:02.620
if you need some.

